<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harvester case</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Harvester judgment</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Harvester case of 1907 was a decision of Justice H. B. Higgins of the Australian Commonwealth Court of Conciliation and Arbitration concerning H. V. McKay's Sunshine Harvester works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>. Higgins ruled that a fair and reasonable wage must meet the needs of a labourer supporting a family, setting a basic wage of seven shillings a day that became the foundation of Australian wage fixing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…federal legislation created a distinctive social settlement: the Immigration Restriction Act of 1901, the basis of the White Australia policy, excluded most non-European migrants, while tariffs protected manufacturing and compulsory arbitration, given force by the <b>Harvester</b> judgement of 1907, set a basic wage intended to support a family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
